  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A strong system today through tomorrow is \nexpected to bring widespread rain with some snow over the north\ntoday/tonight and strong winds tonight into tomorrow.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nUpper trough digging into the upr Midwest will phase with s/wv \nlifting out of the central Plains and deepen sfc low moving twd \nthe Great Lakes today. \n\nSfc high will build into ern Canada by daybreak allowing return\nflow to commence over the region. Isentropic lift looks to \nbring pcpn into wrn zones around 12z, overspreading remainder of\nthe region within 2-3 hours. Given how quickly precip will move\nin cannot rule out light snow falling over the north at onset. \nAccums up to one inch over the higher terrain with a dusting to \n1/2 inch possible over northern Aroostook this morning. As warm \nadvection continues on increasing LLJ temps throughout the CWA \nwill warm and switch snow over to rain over all locations by \n19z. Cannot rule out a mix with sleet as it does so but sfc \ntemps should rapidly rise with icing not expected. \n\nTemps will climb into the 50s south and 40s north by the \nevening hours and continue to rise through midnight before CAA \nmoves into the northwest and drops south during the course of \nthe overnight. By daybreak expect that temps may be down into \nthe u20s over the North Woods and around 40 for Downeast. Precip\nwill be on its way out late and will likely end as rain for \nmost of the area. Some showers are possible in north and west \nareas throughout the day Saturday, which could fall as a \nrain/snow mix. \n\nSoutherly winds on today will gust upwards of 25-30 mph in the \nafternoon as LLJ kicks in. As cold front moves through expect \nwinds to quickly veer to the northwest with frequent gusts \nupwards of 30-35mph overnight, higher in the elevated terrain. \nRapidly falling overnight temps will combine with strong winds \nto produce wind chills in the upper teens and lower 20s over the\nnorth by Saturday morning lasting through Sunday morning. Gusty\nwinds could also cause some isolated power outages.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Extended dry period from the weekend through at\nleast late next week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nAs the system departs Saturday, high pressure will build in over\nthe area. High pressure is expected to continue to dominate\nthrough next week as the jet stream generally stays to our\nnorth. High pressure will limit precipitation chances through\nnext week and lead to seasonable temperatures with highs\ngenerally in the 50s and lows in the 30s.",
